1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,399
Your backtest and your live system are usually two different codebases.

2
00:00:03,449 --> 00:00:04,931
That's why they drift.

3
00:00:05,283 --> 00:00:07,464
With Wingfoil you describe it once, in Rust.

4
00:00:07,514 --> 00:00:13,000
For example: keep an order book, fan out to each side of the top, then recombine them into a quote.

5
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:14,592
Under the hood it's a graph.

6
00:00:14,642 --> 00:00:20,533
The engine runs each node once per cycle, even where branches recombine, at about twenty-seven nanoseconds each.

7
00:00:20,583 --> 00:00:24,700
Run it historically, and it's an instant, deterministic replay for backtests.

8
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:27,558
Run the same graph live, and it's paced by the wall clock.

9
00:00:27,608 --> 00:00:29,127
Same values, same order.

10
00:00:29,177 --> 00:00:31,000
Only the clock changes.

11
00:00:31,050 --> 00:00:32,221
One definition.

12
00:00:32,271 --> 00:00:33,500
Two run modes.

13
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:35,694
The implementations can't drift.

14
00:00:36,183 --> 00:00:37,145
Wingfoil.

15
00:00:37,195 --> 00:00:39,185
Stream processing in Rust.
